# Deep Learning for Arrhythmia Classification: Tackling Class Imbalance in MIT-BIH Database

## 1. Introduction and Background

The MIT-BIH Arrhythmia Database has been the gold standard for ECG arrhythmia research since its introduction in 1980. With 48 half-hour recordings from 47 subjects, it contains approximately 110,000 expert-annotated heartbeat samples. The fundamental challenge? A massive class imbalance - normal beats (N) constitute roughly 83% of the data, while pathological conditions like ventricular ectopic beats (V) represent less than 7%.

Recent work by Kachuee et al. (2018) achieved 93.4% accuracy using a simple CNN on balanced subsets, but this doesn't reflect real-world distributions. Acharya et al. (2017) hit 94.03% with deeper networks but relied on augmentation. Our approach questions whether we actually need these preprocessing gymnastics or if smart architecture choices can handle the natural imbalance.

